(project pricklypear-security
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: SECURITY — the posture, stated once, across three horizons. LOW: the concrete capability/auth/data boundaries as they stand in the kernel today, with the September-2026 hardening pass as the receipt. MID: the agent-era threat model that arrives with LLM callers — untrusted content, the injection-to-effect path, budgets as a security property, and why countermeasure must never become retaliation. HIGH: the long-horizon model — attacker abundance, network partition, issuer-gated identity, redundant residence, and the cactus doctrine (do not defeat the desert; become unworth biting). This chapter is DOCTRINE, not a mechanism chapter: it owns the threat model and the laws. Each mechanism it names is owned by the chapter that implements it (auth.borg, image.borg, one-tree.borg, local-first.borg, prim-seam.borg, agent-native.borg, branching.borg).")
 (status partial)
 (* agent note (|2026-09-27 chapter created from the security wargame discussion (roerick + agent). Intent: one place that says what the security model IS, why each layer exists, and what the next decade does to it — so future capability reviews have a stated baseline to argue against instead of re-deriving it per feature. The instantiation record is docs/security-review-2026-09.md (point-in-time code review keyed by file:line; kept as the receipt, not the spec).|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-09-27 FIXED SINCE docs/security-review-2026-09.md (do not re-report these as live): 1 sort is now a validated grammar (image/lib/crud.ml safe_sort_sql, i-1788669685 — the raw ' ORDER BY ' ^ s oracle is closed). 2 code-restore/undo are gated (image/lib/prims_code.ml: deny_anon + branch_owner_authorized BEFORE branch lookup, i-1788669791 — the promote-bypass is closed). 3 effect inference fails CLOSED at policy gates (lisp/lib/effect.ml strict_prim_effects raises Unresolved_name; unclassified names infer EffSet.top, denied by every allowlist; Session.make fails boot on catalog drift via classification_errors). 4 public-route dispatch RE-GATES (image/lib/session.ml call_handler re-runs Route_gate.check_public_handler per request and sets public_dispatch to deny meta-eval prims, i-1788669715). 5 load-library validates its name (image/lib/loader.ml valid_library_name, ^[a-z0-9_-]{1,64}$ — the traversal read is closed). 6 sleep is classified Net, not pure (lisp/lib/effect.ml net_prims, i-1788669886), so policy gates deny it; example_eval was already strict. See §low-level and §open-gaps for what remains.|) *)
 (* agent note (|2026-09-27 NAMING: this chapter is about PRICKLYPEAR (the OS). The language is Nopales. When prose means the language, say Nopales; when it means the running multi-user image, say pricklypear/the habitat/the image. The 'cactus' metaphor throughout is deliberate and load-bearing (see §cactus) — it is a design stance, not decoration: the system survives by being cheap, spiny, and unproductive to eat, not by winning fights.|) *)

 (convention ocaml-dune)

 ;; ============================================================
 ;;  HORIZON 0 — THE LAWS
 ;; ============================================================

 (section laws
  (doc "Five laws that every mechanism below must satisfy. These are the chapter's actual output; the horizons are the argument for them. If a future feature violates one of these, the feature is wrong until the law is amended in this chapter — same discipline as the frozen prim set in lisp-interpreter.borg.")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
    LAW 1 — STRUCTURE OVER JUDGMENT.
    Every security property that matters lives where violating it
    FAILS STRUCTURALLY — the effect buckets that fail boot on drift,
    the RLS floor in Postgres, branch ownership, the human promote
    gate, the prim catalog. No property may depend on an LLM (or a
    human) choosing correctly. An instruction is a preference; a
    failing boot is a control. Corollary: "the agent is told not to"
    is never a control.

    LAW 2 — NO AGENT HOLDS AN IRREVERSIBLE TRIGGER.
    Agents and automated loops may SENSE, CORROBORATE, CONTAIN and
    RECOMMEND. They may not PROMOTE, MERGE, DELETE, SPEND, or EGRESS
    irreversibly. Irreversibility is the scarce thing; the human is
    the only component that cannot be prompt-injected, so the human
    holds every one-way door.

    LAW 3 — UNTRUSTED INPUT NEVER CARRIES AUTHORITY.
    Content is content. A note body, a scraped page, a peer's synced
    subtree, a defector's report, a room message — none of it may be
    treated as instructions by an actor with effects. Reading
    content and obeying content are the same operation to an LLM;
    the boundary must therefore be structural (taint + effect
    gating), never a matter of the model noticing.

    LAW 4 — REVERSIBLE BY DEFAULT; THE BEFORE-IMAGE IS THE SAFETY NET.
    tree-time gives every write a before-image. Any write path that
    deletes before it can re-create must validate first and
    write-then-sweep (notes.storage SAVE ORDERING CONTRACT, landed
    c03ef04 after a note-destroying incident). It must NOT treat
    history as permission to be careless.

    LAW 5 — SOVEREIGNTY IS THE LAST PERIMETER.
    Any identity can take its signed subtree, leave, and run it
    somewhere else — offline, forever, without asking. Everything
    else (isolation, capability, provenance) exists to make that
    exit meaningful. A system you cannot leave is not yours.
   |))
  )

 ;; ============================================================
 ;;  HORIZON 1 — LOW: THE CONCRETE POSTURE
 ;; ============================================================

 (section low-level
  (doc "The boundaries as they exist in code: what an authenticated caller, an anonymous visitor, a route handler, and a delegated agent can each actually reach — and which layers are structural. Receipt: docs/security-review-2026-09.md (point-in-time). Fixes since that review are recorded in the chapter agent note above; the OPEN items are in §open-gaps.")
  (status partial)
  (implements "image/lib/session.ml" "lisp/lib/effect.ml" "image/lib/crud.ml" "image/lib/auth_store.ml" "migrations/033_admin_rls.sql" "image/lib/example_eval.ml" "image/lib/loader.ml" "image/lib/prims_code.ml")

  (subsection effect-classification
   (doc "Capability is declared, not inferred. every registered prim belongs to exactly one bucket (pure / read-db / write-db / branch / publish / html / net / ai / txn); Session.make audits the catalog at boot against lisp/lib/effect.ml classification_errors and FAILS BOOT on drift. Policy gates (public-route publish, public-route dispatch, AI install) resolve names through strict_prim_effects, where an unclassified name infers EffSet.top — denied by every allowlist, fail closed, with Unresolved_name naming the offender. The 2026-09-10 full sweep (i-1788669715 / i-1788670318) is why eval-string, tree/upsert, tree/delete, link/unlink, sync/*, lens/fork, media/*, tokens, tcp-connect and friends no longer sail through as 'pure'.")
   (status implemented))

  (subsection meta-eval-denial
   (doc "eval-string / load-string / load-library are the capability-reset escape hatch: they evaluate in the live session env with the full prim table and a fresh budget, so publish-time inference can never contain them. Two independent defenses: (1) they are classified into the deny-by-default bucket so gates never trust them; (2) during public-route handler dispatch, t.public_dispatch is set for the duration and those prims raise 'denied in public-route handler dispatch'. Public-route handlers are re-gated at EVERY request (image/lib/session.ml call_handler), not only at (route … 'public) publish time — so a later re-definition cannot re-point a gated route to an ungated body (closes the §2 TOCTOU).")
   (status implemented))

  (subsection data-floor
   (doc "The datum isolation floor is Postgres RLS, not application SQL: tree_data_rows/tree_nodes predicate on the pp.user GUC against data.u.<user>.* plus member spaces plus an admin disjunct (migration 033, tree_user_is_admin). The app-level visible_sql mirror in image/lib/crud.ml changes WITH the policy so reads never drift from the floor. Parameterized SQL only; the one raw ORDER BY path (sort) is now a validated grammar (safe_sort_sql) since the 2026-09 read-oracle finding. Author is bound from the session on create and never rebound on update — admin maintenance keeps attribution.")
   (status implemented))

  (subsection code-write-authorization
   (doc "Code writes (define / route / deftype / tree upserts / code-restore / undo) are gated by branch ownership, not by RLS — RLS deliberately exempts non-datum kinds (migration 013), so the guard must be explicit at the prim. branch_owner_authorized is admin-only for system/* and own-tenant otherwise, checked BEFORE any branch lookup. This is the promote gate's lower half: an identity may time-travel or rewrite only inside its own garden.")
   (status implemented))

  (subsection auth-and-sessions
   (doc "argon2id PHC champion with transparent legacy sha256$ verify-and-rewrite; constant-time compare; dummy-hash timing flatten for unknown usernames; per-username sliding-window throttle with capped exponential backoff (auth_throttle.ml, PP_LOGIN_* knobs, pure/in-memory/one Eio domain). Session tokens and API tokens stored SHA-256-hashed (never the raw secret). Delegated on-behalf-of tokens: bearer carries the AGENT, effects run as the OWNER with actor provenance — DATA ONLY (no define/route/load-library-of-non-seeded/reseed/deftype/checkout/fork/merge), and NO admin capability even when the owner is admin. Revocation is per-token and takes effect on the holder's next request.")
   (status implemented))

  (subsection http-surface
   (doc "Hard-public is a fixed short list (login, health, logout, welcome, book.pdf, src.tgz, /code, /assets, /.well-known/agent.json, /agent.txt); everything else needs a session or Basic. Kernel paths (/data, /admin, /api/eval, /api/prim, …) are hard-private regardless of route registration. Identity attaches BEFORE route classification so a handler can never run as the wrong manifest; subdomain gardens confine; oversized bodies 413 with a per-type media allowlist (SVG forced to attachment); generic 500s never echo exceptions. Security headers today: nosniff, X-Frame-Options DENY, referrer-policy, and CSP limited to frame-ancestors 'none' — NOT a full default-src CSP (the chrome relies on inline style/script), and no CSRF token on browser-driven kernel endpoints. Both are named in §open-gaps.")
   (status partial))

  (subsection sandbox-precedent
   (doc "image/lib/example_eval.ml is the model every other gate is measured against: a fail-closed whitelist (absent prim = absent), a static effect allowlist, blocked net/sleep/meta-eval BEFORE the allow lists, a wall-clock cap, a child env, and Budget_exceeded re-raised through guard. When in doubt about how to gate a new execution surface, copy this file's shape.")
   (status implemented))

  (verify
   (test "boot: Session.make asserts every registered prim is in exactly one bucket (classification_errors non-empty fails boot)")
   (test "public route: a handler re-pointed to a write body is denied at dispatch, not only at publish")
   (test "eval-string/load-string refuse inside public-route dispatch")
   (test "RLS: a non-member identity sees zero rows of another user's data.u subtree, including through find-rows-author")
   (test "code-restore on system/main by a non-admin is denied by branch_owner_authorized before lookup")
   (test "delegated token: define/route/reseed/checkout denied; create/update of the owner's data rows succeeds with author=owner, current-actor=agent"))
 )

 ;; ============================================================
 ;;  HORIZON 2 — MID: THE AGENT-ERA THREAT MODEL
 ;; ============================================================

 (section mid-level
  (doc "What changes when the caller is an LLM loop with tools, and later a population of them. The attacker's scarce resource is no longer knowledge of your code — it is access to an effect channel plus untrusted content in the same loop. This horizon owns the threat model; the mechanisms it requires are mostly OPEN and listed in §open-gaps.")
  (status partial)

  (subsection lethal-trifecta
   (doc "An agent becomes dangerous when one loop holds all three of: (1) private data (data.u.<user>, notes, media, an owner's LLM key), (2) untrusted content (a room message, an ingested page, a synced peer subtree, a defector report), and (3) an effect channel (write prims, http-get/post, ntfy, submit-job, a glochid shell tool). agent-native bots are exactly this shape by design — they wake on cron, read member-gated rooms, and act under their own identity. The mitigation is not 'make the agent careful'; it is to break the triangle structurally: scope the identity's capability so that no single loop holds all three. In particular, an agent that must ingest untrusted content should run with NO egress, and an agent with egress should ingest only authored/verified content.")
   (status planned))

  (subsection taint
   (doc "The mechanism the trifecta mitigation needs: track the PROVENANCE of every value in an eval, and refuse effectful prims when a value flows from an untrusted source. one-tree already gives the ingredients — every node has an author and a content hash, and 027's hash domain makes tampering detectable. A taint flag on the eval context (set when a read crosses into content the session did not author and that is not hash-verified from a trusted root) plus strict_prim_effects-style enforcement at the effect boundary would turn 'don't act on scraped content' from a prompt into a control. This is the single highest-value MID mechanism and it is not built.")
   (status planned))

  (subsection budgets-are-security
   (doc "The quiet tick (wake-loop: no provider call when nothing changed) is a SECURITY property, not an efficiency trick. The cheapest attack on a habitat full of LLM users is not exfiltration — it is a BILL: make noise, trigger wakes, burn fifty humans' provider keys. Therefore: standing cost near zero, hard per-identity provider/token budgets, a hard wake-storm ceiling, and a cap on accumulated db_ms per eval. Complement: the eval has a STEP budget but NO wall-clock deadline (only example_eval has one), and sleep is now correctly classified Net (denied by gates) but with-transaction still wraps arbitrary lambdas — so a txn body holding a slow prim can pin a pgx connection and SERIALIZABLE txn, and the anonymous session is shared. See §open-gaps.")
   (status partial))

  (subsection origin-authenticated-verbs
   (doc "Raw-HTML-on-a-user-route is the product (render-html is the app model); the consequence is that any identity's public page is same-origin script under a visitor's cookie. /api/eval accepts the session cookie, and HttpOnly+SameSite=Lax does not stop a same-site script from fetching it. With CSP limited to frame-ancestors, one-click impersonation via an injected same-site script is available to any authenticated user; PP_BASE_DOMAIN subdomain scoping widens the reach. The fix is to authenticate the ORIGIN of state-changing browser requests: a full default-src 'self' CSP (nonce'd where inline is required) plus a CSRF token or a required custom header on kernel endpoints. OPEN.")
   (status planned))

  (subsection countermeasure-not-retaliation
   (doc "Defensive action must be contained, reversible, verified, and target the attacker's INPUT rather than their infrastructure. PERMITTED (countermeasure): canaries/markers served to unknown visitors and recorded as ordinary append-only rows (attribution you can later corroborate against hashes); tarpits and slow/large responses; context poisoning aimed at a scraper's own reasoning loop; quarantining a room, disarming a glochid node, revoking a delegated token, throttling, filing abuse reports. FORBIDDEN (retaliation): reaching out to strike a party you INFERRED from content. Retaliation is the single most hijackable authority you could ever grant a swarm — an attacker's cheapest move is to serve a sentence naming a target, and if the decision flows through an LLM it is injectable. It is also irreversible, externally unverifiable, breaks the cactus property, and permanently widens every future capability review. LAW 2 is the enforcement: no agent holds that trigger; a human may act offensively under their own name and liability, on signed evidence. NOTE: attacker swarms are MORE injectable than this habitat, because their coordination is natural language between stateless processes that cannot distinguish data from orders, and they have no analogue of data_rows.author provenance or the hash domain. Prompt injection is therefore both a weapon we may wield against their input and a wound they cannot close — and the same asymmetry is aimed at us, which is why LAW 3 is symmetric.")
   (status planned))

  (subsection quorum-and-groupthink
   (doc "Defensive quorum counts VERIFIED-DISTINCT identities, never node count or vote count. Fifty instances of one agent on one model with one prompt are ONE opinion with fifty copies of the same blind spot; a single successful injection that owns one owns all of them simultaneously. Independence requires distinct keys, ideally distinct models/prompts/roles. Byzantine framing: assume k of n are compromised and size the threshold from that. Redundancy buys AVAILABILITY; only independence buys CORROBORATION — do not conflate them.")
   (status planned))

  (subsection distrust-by-default
   (doc "Everything crossing a trust boundary is data, never instruction: a peer's synced subtree, a defector's report, an LLM's tool output, a scraped page. Defector output specifically is content for humans and static analysis only — never fed as instructions into a defensive agent loop, because doing so installs the attacker's injection channel inside the defense. This is LAW 3 applied to our own reconnaissance.")
   (status planned))
 )

 ;; ============================================================
 ;;  HORIZON 3 — HIGH: ABUNDANCE, PARTITION, ISSUER-GATE
 ;; ============================================================

 (section high-level
  (doc "Three futures, one posture. (A) attacker supply goes to infinity with agent swarms; (B) the network becomes unreliable or repeatedly partitioned; (C) identity becomes permissioned (attestation / proof-of-personhood / jurisdiction firewalls). The design responses overlap almost completely, and the substrate already has most of the primitives.")
  (status planned)

  (subsection abundance
   (doc "When attacker supply is infinite, every control LINEAR IN ATTACKER COUNT fails by arithmetic, not by bug: an intentionally expensive verifier (argon2id per Basic-auth request) becomes a self-DoS; per-identity limits are defeated by holding more identities; free anonymity means an unbounded attacker pool. The inversions required: (1) prefer asymmetric verification — expensive to forge, CHEAP to verify (signatures, cached verified identities behind short-TTL scoped tokens) so the second request costs a hash, not half a second; (2) make the limiter's unit THE RESOURCE, not the identity — this branch's write budget, this store's connection, this runner's step budget — binding regardless of who asks; (3) price anonymity (cheap for a person, expensive for a fleet). The posture shifts from PREVENTION to GRACEFUL DEGRADATION: assume a fraction of traffic is adversarial always, and make the blast radius and the heal time the metrics. This is where tree-time pays off: reversibility is a security control, so only irreversible acts (promote, delete, egress, spend) are worth gating hard.")
   (status planned))

  (subsection partition
   (doc "In a partitioned world the failure mode is not downtime — partition is the ATTACKER'S TOOL. Cut a host from the sync index and you can make it accept stale state, fail open, or accept a hostile peer's version on reconnect. Therefore: boot, auth, reads and writes must never depend on the network (the single-image property is a security invariant, not just an ops convenience); the merge is a TRUST BOUNDARY, not a UX detail (local-first.borg sync-conflicts is a security surface); and merges must be verifiable — signed, content-addressed manifests so a peer is trusted because a signature and hash check out, not because a sync service vouched for it. The 027 hash domain is the substrate; signing it is the missing step.")
   (status planned))

  (subsection issuer-gate
   (doc "If network access requires proof-of-personhood or platform attestation, an ISSUER becomes a boot dependency and revocation becomes coercion. Non-negotiable responses: attestations are cached, signed, locally verifiable and expiring, and gate SOME ingress — never boot, never reads, never the local loop; the credential is one layer, never the root of authority (the root is a self-certifying key the person holds); identity is per-context (a key for this habitat, another for that one) so a compromise is not total and correlation is not free; and design for EXIT and VERIFIABILITY — the ability to prove what happened without trusting the platform. The security question inverts: the most powerful attacker may be the party you DEPEND on.")
   (status planned))

  (subsection residences
   (doc "Redundant residence: an agent is not a process and not a machine. Identity is a key, memory is a branch, coordination is the one tree — so an agent is a key plus a subtree, and ANY node that can authenticate as that key and read that subtree can BE that agent. Residence becomes a deployment decision: the same agent can run on a home server, a hosted node, a phone, a laptop, or a van, with capability scoped PER BODY (the home node gets full prims and glochid shell; a hosted node gets read-only; a phone gets data-only). That is LAW 1's least-privilege made structural — enforced by WHERE it runs, not by agent judgment. What this requires and does not yet have: (a) CUSTODY — one residence holds a lease per branch at a time, fenced by a monotonic generation the store enforces (tree_branches def_gen / tree_branch_def_gen is the natural counter; a wall clock is not trustworthy under partition); take the lease at gen g, reject writes from a stale gen. Without fencing, two residences split-brain and double-spend — the known manual-wake/cron-tick double-scan race is the toy version of this. (b) PER-RESIDENCE TOKENS — each body gets its own delegated token so a stolen laptop is revocable without killing the identity; the identity key never leaves the human's custody except to mint. (c) MEMORY AS ROWS — today the agent's memory lives in the provider session (ai_store), so moving residence means amnesia (the 200k-token overflow that minted a fresh session is the worked example); memory must become tree state with the provider session a disposable cache. (d) SNAPSHOT-PINNED EFFECTS — an effect records the generation it was decided against, so a residence on a stale/partitioned view cannot act on a world that never existed. Substrate already present: sync/plan + sync/push (three-way, base = last-synced), migration 032 sync_bases merge index, 028 def_gen, migration 027 hash domain, tree_versions before-images, per-token revocation. Missing: custody, per-residence tokens, memory-as-rows, pinned effects.")
   (status planned))

  (subsection convergent-replication
   (doc "Gossip has no common ancestor: two peers that have never met share no base, and a two-way diff can detect divergence but cannot resolve it. Options: causal ordering (Usenet-style ancestors) or convergence by construction — declare PER-KIND merge semantics and put them in the type system rather than in policy: notes/outlines union-and-order, settings LWW with a logical clock, genuinely ordered/chained things conflict-and-surface. tree_versions' monotonic generation axis plus content hashing are the ingredients. This is also where eclipse and stale-state poisoning are answered: signed manifests with monotonic counters, independent hash verification, peer-diversity requirements, and the rule that an OLD VALID signature is still a weapon unless a generation travels with it.")
   (status planned))

  (subsection signing
   (doc "The single missing cryptographic layer: sign branch roots and merged manifests with a node/identity key so tree/get reads and sync merges can be verified without asking anyone. tree_nodes already carries content hashes computed DB-side in one domain (migration 027); wrapping them in signatures yields self-certifying branch roots, verifiable merges, and an offline-verifiable audit trail. This is the prerequisite for §partition, §residences, and §exit — it is the highest-leverage HIGH item.")
   (status planned))

  (subsection exit
   (doc "Sovereignty is LAW 5. The concrete form: a signed, single-file export of an identity's subtree that a fresh image can ingest entirely offline — src.tgz plus a signed tree dump — so leaving the habitat never requires a network or anyone's permission. Every other property (isolation, capability, provenance) exists to make this exit meaningful. A system you cannot leave is not yours, and a system whose owner cannot leave has no security model worth the name.")
   (status planned))

  (subsection van
   (doc "The mobile-habitat corollary, and the reason the whole substrate is shaped this way: a single image on a battery in a van IS the complete system, not a degraded one (bind anywhere; Postgres local; htmx/all UI local; boot network-independent). A mesh (addresses derived from public keys, self-certifying, no DNS/CA/registry to coerce, no bootstrap server to knock offline) makes physical adjacency the handshake: meeting in person is the out-of-band channel, minting a scoped expiring token is the handshake, and store-and-forward (UUCP/Usenet prior art) is the transport. Consequence stated plainly: the internet becomes ONE of several links and the least trustworthy one; the desert is the perimeter and the spines are cryptographic.")
   (status planned))

  (subsection cactus
   (doc "The doctrine named for the plant, and the one-line answer to 'what do you do about infinite attackers': do not defeat the desert; become unworth biting. A cactus survives by being metabolically cheap, hard to traverse, low-value per bite, and replaceable as a KIND even when an individual dies. Translated: cheap idle metabolism (quiet ticks, no provider call without an event); spiny traversal (content-addressed, hash-domained, versioned, RLS-floored data — expensive to extract and tamper-evident); no honey pot (no central credential store that opens everything; LLM keys sealed per user; value never concentrated); and replaceability (identity is a key that outlives any body; the agent is a species, not a server). The defense is not a fight — it is an immune system: dormant and cheap, local sentinels report, independent quorum corroborates, cheap reversible measures fire automatically (quarantine, revoke, throttle, alarm), and every irreversible measure queues for a human. The one-sentence form: the cactus does not defeat the desert; it becomes so cheap, so spiny, and so unproductive to eat that the desert stops trying.")
   (status planned))
 )

 ;; ============================================================
 ;;  OPEN GAPS
 ;; ============================================================

 (section open-gaps
  (doc "The honest queue. Unless noted, each is OPEN as of the chapter's creation; the fixed items are recorded in the top agent note. Items that need spec work belong in the owning chapter; items that are bugs belong in the habitat log/issues register (habitat-workflow FILE DON'T FIX).")
  (status partial)
  (details
   (doc (|
    LOW / hardening
    - Wall-clock deadline: the eval has a STEP budget, not a
      wall-clock cap (only example_eval has one). A single expensive
      prim is one step. Add an Eio timeout/cancellation wrapping
      request eval, with prim boundaries as cancellation points.
    - sleep inside with-transaction with no wall-clock cap can pin a
      pgx connection + SERIALIZABLE txn; the anonymous session is
      SHARED, so a public page can freeze the public surface. Deny
      net prims inside txn bodies (or cap txn wall-time), and cap
      accumulated db_ms per eval.
    - CSP is frame-ancestors 'none' only; no CSRF token / required
      custom header on browser-driven kernel endpoints (/api/eval is
      reachable with the session cookie). See §origin-authenticated-verbs.
    - Per-IP pre-auth limiting is absent; argon2id verify runs per
      Basic-auth request; the throttle table (4096, LRU-evict) lets a
      username flood evict a real user's failure window. Consider
      verify-once + short-TTL scoped token.
    - LLM keys are envelope-sealed ONLY when PP_LLM_ENVELOPE_KEY is
      set; otherwise stored plaintext with a loud warning. Consider
      requiring the key (fail closed) in prod.
    - Egress DNS-rebinding TOCTOU (resolve then curl re-resolves);
      pin the checked IP or proxy egress.
    - Anonymous session env is shared mutable state; consider a fresh
      child env per public request.

    MID / agent-era (the mechanisms, not the hardening)
    - §taint is unbuilt: untrusted-content provenance to effect
      refusal. Highest-value MID item.
    - §budgets-are-security: per-identity provider-spend caps and a
      wake-storm ceiling.
    - Canary layer: no marker/attribution surface exists today.
    - Defensive quorum: independence and threshold policy undefined.

    HIGH / future
    - §signing (prerequisite for everything below it).
    - §residences: custody/lease with store-enforced fencing,
      per-residence tokens, memory-as-rows, snapshot-pinned effects.
    - §convergent-replication: per-kind merge semantics in the type
      system; conflict policy remains LWW-v0 report-only.
    - §exit: signed offline export/ingest path.
   |))
  )
  (verify
   (test "grep check: no section claims a mechanism is built unless the cited module exists and is referenced by an (implements …) or a live prim")
   (smoke "after each hardening item lands, the §low-level verify block still passes")
  )
 )
 )
)